00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
in today's tutorial I will show you how to&nbsp;
prepare an image for print using Photoshop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:13.800
let's get started in order to send an image to&nbsp;
print your image needs to be high resolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:22.080
so what that means is that your image must&nbsp;
be set up at 300 dots per inch minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:33.720
and it must be set up as CMYK color profile&nbsp;
CMYK stands for cyan magenta yellow and black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:42.720
and when I set my file up for print and CMYK&nbsp;
I am separating the image into those four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.660
colors so when this image is sent to the&nbsp;
printer it will print in those four colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.340
so to get started prepping this for print let's&nbsp;
go ahead and change our image size and make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:08.100
our file is set up at 300 dots per inch or 300&nbsp;
DPI go to image size you'll get a pop-up here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:16.140
on the right I can see my image resolution&nbsp;
here I can see the dimensions and the size

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.280
the number that I really need to pay attention&nbsp;
to is the resolution this image was downloaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.720
from a free stock photo website so it was&nbsp;
uploaded to the web therefore it is web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:40.800
resolution which is 72 DPI I need to change this&nbsp;
number to 300. so I'm just going to type 300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:49.260
you'll notice when I did that some of these other&nbsp;
sizes changed that's because I'm essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:59.640
reducing the size of the image by adding these&nbsp;
pixels you never want to have resample clicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:06.960
here resample would essentially blow up your image&nbsp;
and make it make it really pixelated so now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:16.260
we've set this at 300 this is still a really high&nbsp;
resolution file 20 inches by 11.25 inches that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.980
pretty big image so this file from a resolution&nbsp;
standpoint looks good I'm going to click ok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.300
last thing we need to do is&nbsp;
change the image from RGB to CMYK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.760
you can actually see the color mode in the tab&nbsp;
in Photoshop I can see this image is still RGB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:49.260
so it should change it to CMYK go to&nbsp;
image mode and let's switch it to CMYK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.460
you might get a pop-up box here that asks you are&nbsp;
you sure that you actually want to convert it if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:03.660
you do just say yes okay so we have changed the&nbsp;
resolution to 300 DPI we have changed the color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:11.460
mode to CMYK this image should be ready for print&nbsp;
so now I'm going to save a copy of this image I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:21.300
don't want to override my original image so let's&nbsp;
go to file save a copy I'm going to add the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:27.420
print into my file name just so I know this&nbsp;
file is the correct one that is going to print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:36.240
have it set up as a jpeg and let's click save you&nbsp;
may get a pop-up that asks about image quality you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.940
always for print ready image you want to make sure&nbsp;
that the slider is slid all the way to the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:49.740
that we have maximum image quality okay that&nbsp;
is how you prepare an image to your friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

